---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Chip from '../components/chip.astro';
import { Icon } from 'astro-icon/components';

import '../styles/project.css';
---

<Layout 
	title="LLM Data Stream Parsers | Vincent Sanicolas" 
	description="Multi-format incremental stream parsers for Python, TypeScript, Dart, C#, and Kotlin. Supporting real-time reactive parsing of JSON, YAML, and XML."
	showBack={true}
>
	<!-- Title Header with Links Below Subtitle -->
	<div data-snap="block" class="show-snap project-detail-header" data-snap-align="fill">
		<div class="header-content">
			<span class="project-mono-label">MULTI-LANGUAGE & MULTI-FORMAT UTILITY</span>
			<h1>LLM Data Stream Parsers</h1>
			<p class="subtitle">An advanced suite of incremental parsers supporting JSON, YAML, and XML streams across Python, TypeScript, Dart, C#, and Kotlin ecosystems.</p>
			
			<div class="project-links">
				<Chip label="Python">
					<Icon name="simple-icons:python" slot="icon" />
				</Chip>
				<Chip label="TypeScript">
					<Icon name="simple-icons:typescript" slot="icon" />
				</Chip>
				<Chip label="Dart">
					<Icon name="simple-icons:dart" slot="icon" />
				</Chip>
				<Chip label="C#">
					<Icon name="simple-icons:csharp" slot="icon" />
				</Chip>
				<Chip label="Kotlin">
					<Icon name="simple-icons:kotlin" slot="icon" />
				</Chip>
			</div>
		</div>
	</div>

	<!-- Centered Wider Content Container -->
	<div class="project-content-container">
		
		<!-- Overview with Inline Wrapping Image -->
		<div data-snap="block" class="show-snap section-block overview-section" data-snap-align="fill">
			<div class="overview-flow-wrapper">
				<div class="project-image-container hero-image-container">
					<img src="/images/llm-data-stream-parsers-placeholder.png" alt="LLM Data Stream Parsers Schematic" class="project-image" transition:name="llm-data-stream-parsers-image" />
				</div>
				<h2>The Overview</h2>
				<p>
					<code>llm_data_stream_parsers</code> is an advanced open-source suite of high-performance packages published across five programming language ecosystems: Python (PyPI), TypeScript (npm), Dart (pub.dev), C# (NuGet), and Kotlin (Maven). It is specifically engineered to handle token-by-token text streams generated by Large Language Models, reactively parsing and yielding incomplete JSON, YAML, and XML data structures instantly as they arrive.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>

		<!-- The Problem -->
		<div data-snap="block" class="show-snap section-block" data-snap-align="fill">
			<h2>The Problem</h2>
			<p>
				While JSON is the most common format for structured outputs, many LLMs or specific workflows yield responses in alternative structured configurations like YAML (frequently used for config generation) or XML/HTML tags (commonly seen in chain-of-thought or multi-agent messaging frameworks). Traditional parsers throw syntax exceptions when attempting to evaluate partial files. Waiting for the complete stream destroys the interactive feel of generative applications, forcing users to wait for entire processes to finish before seeing structured updates.
			</p>
		</div>

		<!-- The Solution -->
		<div data-snap="block" class="show-snap section-block" data-snap-align="fill">
			<h2>The Solution</h2>
			<p>
				The <code>llm_data_stream_parsers</code> suite acts as universal reactive middleware. By implementing format-agnostic internal state-machines, it tracks token-level progress, completes incomplete nodes (such as unclosed XML tags, broken YAML lists, or incomplete JSON keys) in memory, and spits out clean, valid, fully usable partial representations of the stream. This powers instant UI updates and seamless progressive rendering for any structured data representation.
			</p>
		</div>

		<!-- Supported Data Formats -->
		<div data-snap="block" class="show-snap section-block" data-snap-align="fill">
			<h2>Supported Formats</h2>
			<p>
				The library includes specialized sub-parsers for the following three formats:
			</p>
			<div class="capabilities-list">
				<div class="capability-item">
					<div class="capability-title">JSON Parser</div>
					<p>Intelligently balances and completes nested objects, array lists, and incomplete strings, keeping structures valid at every frame.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="capability-item">
					<div class="capability-title">YAML Parser</div>
					<p>Resolves indentation-sensitive indentation rules, handling incomplete block lists, mapping pairs, and multiline string blocks dynamically.</p>
				</div>
				<div class="capability-item">
					<div class="capability-title">XML / Markup Parser</div>
					<p>Instantly corrects open tags, auto-creates closing elements, and provides real-time node tree extraction even for heavily nested elements.</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<!-- How to Use It -->
		<div data-snap="block" class="show-snap section-block" data-snap-align="fill">
			<h2>Multi-Language Usage</h2>
			<p class="usage-description">
				Import the parser matching your stack, configure it for your targeted data format, and pipe your stream through it.
			</p>
			
			<div class="code-example-container" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
				<div class="code-tab">
					<Icon name="simple-icons:python" class="code-tab-icon" />
					<span>Python Example</span>
				</div>
				<pre class="code-block"><code>{`from llm_data_stream_parsers import YamlStreamParser

parser = YamlStreamParser()

# Listen to incoming LLM token stream
async for chunk in response_stream:
    partial_yaml = parser.parse_chunk(chunk)
    if partial_yaml:
        await update_application_state(partial_yaml)`}</code></pre>
			</div>

			<div class="code-example-container">
				<div class="code-tab">
					<Icon name="simple-icons:typescript" class="code-tab-icon" />
					<span>TypeScript Example</span>
				</div>
				<pre class="code-block"><code>{`import { XMLStreamParser } from 'llm-data-stream-parsers';

const parser = new XMLStreamParser();

// Reactively parse streaming XML chunks
for await (const chunk of xmlStream) {
  const partialTree = parser.parseChunk(chunk);
  if (partialTree) {
    renderGenerativeUI(partialTree);
  }
}`}</code></pre>
			</div>
		</div>

	</div>
</Layout>
